{"adjusted_val_bpb": 0.08990049532554728, "attempt": 0, "charged": true, "critic": {"issues": ["No sign of gaming the measurement. The candidate does not read files, paste answers or use test labels. It only uses train_smiles and train_y for fitting. test_smiles is used only to make features and predictions.", "No bug that makes the result meaningless. The fingerprint blocks are joined side by side (hstack). Each of the 24 members is trained on a random third of the columns, and the column choice uses seed+member, so it follows the seed rule. Members are combined by rank-averaging within the fold's test set, and the result is rescaled to [0,1]. Rank-based scores leave AUROC unchanged, so this is valid. The outputs are not true probabilities, but that is the same as the incumbent.", "Recomputed z: (0.0906 - 0.0899) / (0.0049 * sqrt(1/1 + 1/2)) = 0.0007 / 0.00600 \u2248 0.117. The harness reports 0.122. The small gap is likely because the shown means are rounded to 4 decimals, so this is not a real mismatch.", "The gain of about 0.0007 is roughly 0.12 of a noise sd, so it is not significant. The decision to reject is consistent with the numbers.", "Cost note: the candidate runs 24 XGBoost fits of 500 trees per fold, 360 over a full job, where the incumbent ran one per fingerprint set. That adds much more compute and code for no measurable gain. Under the simplicity rule this is another reason to reject. It is not a reason to veto."], "ok": true, "veto": false}, "decision": "reject", "description": "Replicate the slot-27 random-subspace XGBoost ensemble unchanged: pool the three feature blocks into one matrix and fit 24 XGBoost members, each on a seeded random third of the columns, then rank-average them.", "edit_class": "structural", "flags": [], "gpu": "walnut", "idea_id": "b031", "incumbent_after": {"mean": 0.0906378145976652, "n": 2}, "incumbent_before": {"mean": 0.0906378145976652, "n": 2}, "job_id": "B2_bbb_HH_2_s0-candidate-15_1795072667101", "kind": "candidate"